                                    content: `<h3>1. Definition (Advanced)</h3>
&bull; When present, figcaption provides the <b>accessible name</b> of the figure element in the accessibility tree.<br>
&bull; It is defined as flow content, so it can hold links, emphasis, lists and even a table.<br>
&bull; It must be the <b>first or last child</b> of the figure. Any other position is a content model error that validators flag.<br>
&bull; The naming order matters: aria-label or aria-labelledby on the figure will override the figcaption.<br>
<h3>2. Purpose of This Topic (Advanced)</h3>
&bull; To give complex images such as charts a visible long description, which alt cannot carry well.<br>
&bull; To store editorial metadata such as credit, licence and source in structured, styleable markup.<br>
&bull; To support numbered cross references in generated documentation and print output.<br>
<h3>3. Need of This Topic (Advanced)</h3>
&bull; Accessibility guidance for data images requires both a short alt and a longer visible explanation.<br>
&bull; CMS and markdown pipelines generate captions automatically, so validation is needed to avoid invalid markup.<br>
&bull; Interview question: how does figcaption interact with alt, aria-labelledby and aria-describedby.<br>`,
