                                    content: `<h3>1. Definition (Advanced)</h3>
&bull; SVG is an XML based vector format with its own DOM. <b>Inline SVG nodes are real DOM elements</b>, so they can be selected, styled and animated like HTML.<br>
&bull; The <b>viewBox</b> defines the user coordinate system, and <b>preserveAspectRatio</b> controls how it maps into the element box. This is what makes svg truly resolution independent.<br>
&bull; Delivery mode changes the capabilities. <b>&lt;img&gt; with an svg file</b> is an isolated document: no external CSS, no script, no external resources. <b>Inline SVG</b> shares the page CSS and JavaScript. <b>&lt;use&gt; with a sprite</b> sits in the middle and has its own CORS rules.<br>
&bull; <b>currentColor</b> is the standard bridge that lets one sprite icon inherit theme colours.<br>
<h3>2. Purpose of This Topic (Advanced)</h3>
&bull; To build a themeable, cacheable icon system with a single sprite request instead of dozens.<br>
&bull; To render data visualisations that stay accessible, since svg text remains real text.<br>
&bull; To ship logos and illustrations that respond to dark mode and to prefers-reduced-motion.<br>
<h3>3. Need of This Topic (Advanced)</h3>
&bull; User uploaded SVG is a known XSS vector, so a sanitisation and serving policy is mandatory.<br>
&bull; Inline sprites versus separate files is a real performance trade off that teams must reason about.<br>
&bull; Interview question: why can CSS not style an svg loaded through &lt;img&gt;.<br>`,
